WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.240
"Are Baruka Nuts
the Healthiest Nut?"

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:13.680
There is a new nut on the
market called baru almonds

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.400
branded as “barukas,” or just baru nuts.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:21.280
Technically, it isn’t a nut but a seed
that's native to the Brazilian Savannah,

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:25.680
known as the Cerrado, which
is sadly now among the most

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.080
threatened ecosystems in the world.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.760
Over the last 30 years, extensive cattle
ranching and feed crop production

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:35.920
to fatten said cattle have
destroyed much of this ecosystem.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:39.760
So, hey, if we can make it profitable
not to cut down the native trees

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.560
by selling baru nuts, for example,
then that could be good

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:46.640
for the ecosystem’s health.
But what about our health?

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.360
Although baru nuts are
popular and widely consumed,

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.400
few studies report on
their biological properties.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.993
They do have a lot of
polyphenol phytonutrients,

00:00:54.993 --> 00:00:58.236
presumably accounting for
their high antioxidant activity,

00:00:58.236 --> 00:01:01.200
and 90%of the phytonutrients
are present in the peel.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.760
So, are they nutritious? Sure.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:07.040
But do they have any
special health benefits

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.480
(and not just for the
treatment of chubby mice)?

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:14.480
Groups fed baru nuts showed lower
cholesterol, supposedly indicating

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:17.120
that they have a great
potential for dietary use in

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:19.360
preventing and controlling
cholesterol problems.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:26.433
But their groups were rats,
and that was compared to lard.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:30.480
Basically anything lowers your
cholesterol compared to eating lard.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:32.720
Nevertheless, there haven’t been
any reports about the effect

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:37.360
of baru almonds consumption on
human health, until this study.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:40.640
A randomized, controlled study
of humans found that eating less

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.925
than an ounce a day for six weeks
led to a 9% drop in LDL cholesterol.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.120
Twenty grams would be
about 15 nuts or a palmful.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:51.680
Like many other nut studies,
even though the research subjects

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:55.920
were told to add nuts to their regular
diets, there was no weight gain,

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.800
presumably because nuts are
so filling, you inadvertently

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:01.528
cut down on other foods
throughout the day.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:04.880
How good is a 9.4% drop in LDL?

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:08.000
Well, that’s the kind of drop
you can get from regular almonds,

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.760
though macadamias and pistachios
may work even better,

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.080
but those were at much higher doses.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:17.440
So, it appeared that 20 grams
of baru nuts worked as well as

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:21.360
73 grams of almonds, and so
on a per serving basis,

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:26.800
or a per calorie basis, baru nuts
really did seem to be special.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:29.440
Now there are lower
dose nut studies showing

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:31.040
similar or even better results.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:34.400
Here people were given 25 grams
of almonds for just four weeks

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.640
and got about a 6% drop.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:39.920
And in this study, people were given
just 10 grams of almonds a day,

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:43.360
I mean we’re talkin’ just seven
individual almonds a day,

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:48.320
and got more like a 30% drop in
the same time frame as the baru nuts.

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:52.247
Three times better at half
the dose with regular almonds.

00:02:52.247 --> 00:02:55.840
The biggest reason we’re
more confident in regular almonds

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:58.000
is that studies have
been done over and over,

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:00.720
more than a dozen
randomized controlled trials,

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.920
whereas in the only other
cholesterol trial of baru nuts

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:08.000
there was no significant benefit
at all for LDL cholesterol,

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.240
even at the same 20-gram
dose given for even longer—

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:12.968
a period of eight weeks.
That’s a bummer.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.000
The primary reason I would
suggest choosing other nuts instead,

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:19.120
though, is that you
can’t get baru nuts raw.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:22.320
They contain certain compounds
that have to be inactivated by heat

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:23.840
before human consumption.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:27.120
So, the reason raw nuts are
preferable is because of advanced

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:29.360
glycation end products (AGEs),
so-called glycotoxins,

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:30.880
which are known to
contribute to increased

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:33.440
oxidative stress and inflammation.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:37.120
Glycotoxins are naturally present
in uncooked animal-derived foods,

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:40.880
but then dry-heat cooking like
grilling can make things worse.

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:45.120
The three highest levels recorded
are bacon, broiled hot dogs,

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:49.621
and roasted barbequed chicken skin
(nothing comes close to that.)

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:54.080
Chicken McNuggets come in here,
but, anyway, any foods high in fat

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:57.440
and protein can create AGEs
at high enough temperatures.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:01.360
So, although plant foods tend
to contain relatively few AGEs,

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:05.360
even after cooking, there are some
high-fat, high-protein plant foods.

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:09.200
For example, boiled tofu,
like in a soup, is down here,

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:13.760
but the same serving size
of broiled tofu is up here.

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:16.480
Now again, with most plant foods
it’s not at all a problem.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:20.080
Like here’s a raw apple,
and here’s a baked apple.

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:23.920
It doesn’t really matter since
it’s not high fat or high protein.

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:26.720
I was surprised that
veggie burgers were so low,

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:28.320
even when baked or fried.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:33.120
But nuts and seeds are up in tofu
territory, especially when roasted,

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:35.600
which is why I would
recommend raw nuts and seeds

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:38.780
and nut and seed butters
whenever you have a choice.

